# Layered Architecture (Java / Spring)

Default synchronous flow:

```text
Client -> Controller -> Request DTO -> Service -> Manager / Domain Logic -> Repository / DAO -> Database
```

External integration: `Service / Manager -> Client / Adapter -> External System`

Not every feature needs every layer. Do not create layers just because the pattern exists.

## Controller
Responsible for HTTP concerns only: endpoints, path/query params, request DTOs, basic validation, authN/authZ, calling a Service, mapping to response DTOs, HTTP status codes.

Never in controllers: business logic, DB queries, direct repository calls, complex object construction, long workflows, external API orchestration.

## Service
Represents an application use case: coordinate the flow, application-level validation, transaction boundaries, call repositories and managers, trigger events, coordinate integrations.
Express *what the application is doing*, not low-level infrastructure. Avoid huge services that hold every capability.

## Manager
A reusable business/domain capability or complex orchestration (e.g. PaymentManager, NotificationManager, PermissionManager).
- Service = use-case orchestration. Manager = reusable business capability.
- Do NOT create a Manager just because a feature "should have one". If a Service is simple, keep the logic there.
- Avoid God Managers with unrelated functionality.

## Repository / DAO
Persistence only: queries, CRUD, filtering, pagination, sorting, efficient retrieval.
- Good: `findActiveUsers(...)`, `findOrdersByCustomerId(...)`
- Bad: `approveUser(...)`, `calculateDiscount(...)` (business decisions belong above persistence)
- Never access the DB directly from controllers.

## Entity / Domain Model
- Entities represent persistence, not the API.
- A separate domain model is optional. Add it only if it gives real benefit.

## Dependency Injection
- Constructor injection with `final` fields.
- Avoid: manual construction of dependencies in business classes, static global state, service locators, hidden dependencies.

## Code Quality
- Prefer composition over inheritance.
- Do not create an interface for every service "by default". Create abstractions only when they add value (multiple implementations, boundaries, testing seams).
- Avoid generic `Utils` classes with unrelated functionality.

## Package Structure (baseline, not mandatory)
```text
controller/  dto/{request,response}/  service/  manager/  repository/  entity/
client/  mapper/  exception/  config/  security/  messaging/{producer,consumer}/
```
If the project already uses a coherent feature-based, clean, or hexagonal structure, follow it instead.
